  "query_preview": "..\nTonight: An upper ridge axis will move overhead resulting in \ndry conditions. Upper slopes and ridges and even parts of the\nBasin will only see moderate humidity recovery overnight. Should\nbe a modest increase in winds on the east slopes of the Cascades\ntonight compared to recent nights. For temperatures, lows will \nbe in the 50s to low 60s. Smoke will settle into same areas as\nlast night, probably most notably \n\nFriday and Saturday: The ridge axis shifts east and weakens and\nbrings a zonal flow pattern. Winds will increase as warm dry \nconditions continue. Winds will be west with sustained speeds \nin the teens and gusts into the 20 to 30 mph range. The \nstrongest winds are most likely to remain confined to portions \nof central Washington including the east Cascade valleys and the\nWaterville Plateau. There is around a 40-70% chance of wind \ngusts up to 30-35 mph in those locations in central WA Friday \nand Saturday. One major difference from August 1 is the lack of \nstrong low level winds. 850-700mb winds are only reaching into \nthe 10-20 mph range compared to the 30-40 mph from August 1. It \nis still an elevated to critical fire weather period due to the \ncontinued hot and dry conditions combined with the breezy \nwinds. Fire Weather Watches were upgraded to Red Flag Warnings\nfor most of the area for breezy westerly winds and low relative\nhumidity both Friday and Saturday. Exception, a Fire Weather\nWatch remains in effect for FWZ702 (Colville Reservation) since\nit is questionable if winds/low RH align enough to warrant an \nupgrade. Highs will be in the upper 80s and 90s with humidity in\nthe teens to low 20s. Overnight lows will be in the 50s to low \n60s. Minimum relative humidity will reach the 10-25% range with \nlimited recovery overnight.\n\nSunday through Tuesday: Cooler air will filter into the region.\nWinds will remain diurnally driven with the potential for some \nlocally breezy winds through the Cascade gaps. There is still no\ndecent chance of precip expected through at least Tuesday. \nHighs will generally be in the 80s with low in the upper 40s and\n50s. \n\nAfter Tuesday: There is more uncertainty in the forecast\nWednesday and beyond since some ensemble members are showing \nincreasing heights, while others bring a deeper PacNW trough.\nPrecip probabilities remain low, but do show ~15-25% chance \nshowers (thunder?), especially across northern zones Wed/Thu. \n-Spilde/JDC",
